[stderr] ## Accessibility and release boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every transition has a visible text state and an equivalent touch, pointer,
[stderr] keyboard, and gamepad action. Focus is visible; pause/resume, timer state,
[stderr] shortage disclosure, save success/failure, selected answer, correct/incorrect,
[stderr] and explanation are never communicated by colour, animation, or audio alone.
[stderr] Timer on/off is labelled, reversible before start, and compatible with reduced
[stderr] motion and muted/captioned feedback. There is no ranked, daily, live, event,
[stderr] account-only, or target-exclusive round mode in this scope.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Release validation requires exactly `TUT-FX-01`–`TUT-FX-08`,
[stderr] `MIX-FX-01`–`MIX-FX-07`, and `LIFE-FX-01`–`LIFE-FX-13`, with each named tutorial
[stderr] card and every lifecycle transition covered above. Published card facts and
[stderr] answer rules remain subject to the editorial/provenance validation contracts.
[stderr] # Score, streak, and results bible
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Scope:** accepted design evidence for `GAME-007`, `GAME-008`, `GPI-010`, and
[stderr] `GPI-011`. This is a deterministic future-core contract, not implementation or
[stderr] production content. It consumes immutable `GAME-003` adjudication outcomes and
[stderr] the frozen run/card sequence from `GPI-009`. A run ID, attempt ID, interaction
[stderr] ID, and completion ID are stable UUID-like opaque values; retries reuse the
[stderr] relevant ID and never derive a second event from timing, UI order, or a network
[stderr] response.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-010 — score and streak contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] An attempt first resolves exactly once to one of `correct`, `wrong`, `blank`,
[stderr] `timeout`, or `abandoned`. `blank` is an explicit confirm with no answer;
[stderr] `timeout` may occur only when the player explicitly chose the enabled timer
[stderr] mode; and `abandoned` records a deliberate abandon command, never an inferred
[stderr] answer from pause, backgrounding, crash, or loss of focus. Pausing and recovery
[stderr] therefore preserve the unresolved attempt and are not scoring outcomes.
[stderr] 
[stderr] `scoreBefore`, `streakBefore`, `scoreDelta`, `streakAfter`, and `scoreAfter`
[stderr] are written to the immutable score event when the attempt resolves. The event
[stderr] is the source of a reopened feedback or result surface; the score is never
[stderr] recomputed from changed content or presentation settings.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Resolved outcome | Score delta | Streak after resolution | Feedback state | Continue/recovery rule |
[stderr] | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 0–1 | +100 | streak before + 1 | `correct_base` | Explanation and continue are available. |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 2 | +125 | 3 | `correct_streak` | First streak bonus is +25; announce the three-in-a-row state. |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 3 | +150 | 4 | `correct_streak` | Bonus rises to +50. |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 4 | +175 | 5 | `correct_streak` | Bonus rises to +75. |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 5 | +200 | 6 | `correct_streak_cap` | The bonus reaches its +100 cap. |
[stderr] | `correct`, streak before 6 or more | +200 | streak before + 1 | `correct_streak_cap` | Bonus stays capped at +100; no further increase. |
[stderr] | `wrong` | +0 | 0 | `incorrect` | Show canonical answer/explanation; player may continue or pause. |
[stderr] | `blank` | +0 | 0 | `blank` | Explain that no answer was submitted; player may continue or pause. |
[stderr] | `timeout` | +0 | 0 | `timeout` | Only valid with frozen `timerMode=on`; show the answer/explanation and continue/pause controls. |
[stderr] | `abandoned` | +0 | 0 | `abandoned` | End this run recoverably; preserve its partial result and offer resume/replay/theme/exit as applicable. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] The active streak has no maximum. After a correct resolution, `bonus =
[stderr] min(100, max(0, 25 × (streakAfter - 2)))`; thus the first three-correct streak
[stderr] adds +25 and every later correct adds another +25 until the +100 cap. A correct
[stderr] answer is worth at most 200 points. `scoreAfter = scoreBefore + scoreDelta`;
[stderr] score is a non-negative monotonic total. `bestActiveSessionStreak` is the
[stderr] maximum resolved correct streak in this run and never falls when the active
[stderr] streak resets. A retry is a new `runId`; it retains aggregate history but never
[stderr] overwrites the prior run's attempts or score events.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Equivalent feedback requirements
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each feedback state exposes the same outcome in four independently useful
[stderr] forms. Assets and cues may decorate these forms but cannot be the only carrier
[stderr] of meaning.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | State | Required text and semantic label | Required non-colour visual | Caption/mute audio equivalent | Reduced-motion behaviour |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `correct_base` | “Correct. +100 points. Streak: N.” | Success icon/pattern plus labelled score panel. | If the correct cue plays, caption `Correct; 100 points; streak N`; mute leaves all text visible. | Static labelled state; no animation required. |
[stderr] | `correct_streak` | “Correct. +[100 + bonus] points. Streak: N. Streak bonus +[25/50/75].” | Success icon/pattern plus a labelled streak marker. | Caption names correct result, points, streak, and bonus; mute changes no score information. | Replace celebration movement with static marker. |
[stderr] | `correct_streak_cap` | “Correct. +200 points. Streak: N. Bonus capped at +100.” | Success icon/pattern plus labelled cap marker. | Caption names capped bonus and points; mute changes no score information. | Static cap marker; no pulsing or forced animation. |
[stderr] | `incorrect`, `blank`, `timeout` | Outcome name, “+0 points”, “Streak reset”, canonical answer, and explanation. | Distinct labelled error/empty/time pattern; not red or animation alone. | Caption names outcome, zero points, reset, answer, and explanation availability; mute leaves this text visible. | Static feedback and controls. |
[stderr] | `abandoned` | “Round ended. No points lost. Streak reset.” plus the recovery/next action. | Distinct labelled exit/interrupted pattern. | Caption names end state and next action; mute leaves this text visible. | Static end state and focusable action. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Screen-reader semantics announce the same text once per resolved attempt. Focus
[stderr] moves to the feedback heading, not an animation. All feedback includes visible
[stderr] continue, pause, and where relevant replay/theme/exit actions with equivalent
[stderr] touch, pointer, keyboard, and gamepad operation.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Score and feedback edge fixtures
[stderr] 
[stderr] All fixtures start from a frozen `Mixed Fixture` run unless stated otherwise.
[stderr] Each `resolve` command has a unique interaction ID, and a repeated command uses
[stderr] the same one.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | ID | Input / action | Expected immutable record and visible outcome |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-01` | Correct with score 0, streak 0. | One event: `+100`, streak 1, score 100, `correct_base`; text/non-colour/caption equivalents agree. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-02` | From streak 1, correct. | One event: `+100`, streak 2, score rises by 100; no bonus message. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-03` | From score 200, streak 2, correct. | One event: `+125`, streak 3, score 325, `correct_streak`; text and caption name +25 bonus. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-04` | From score 325, streak 3, correct. | One event: `+150`, streak 4, score 475; bonus rises to +50. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-05` | From score 475, streak 4, correct. | One event: `+175`, streak 5, score 650; feedback names the +75 bonus. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-06` | From score 650, streak 5, correct; then from streak 6, correct again. | The first event is `+200`, streak 6, score 850, `correct_streak_cap`; the second is `+200`, streak 7, score 1050, proving the +100 cap does not rise. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-07` | From score 1050, streak 7, resolve `wrong`. | One zero-point event, active streak 0, score remains 1050, best streak remains 7; answer/explanation and static non-colour feedback are available. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-08` | From score 1050, streak 4, explicitly submit blank. | One zero-point `blank` event, active streak 0, score remains 1050; no answer is guessed and continue/pause remain available. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-09` | With frozen `timerMode=on`, from score 1050, streak 3, let the enabled timer expire. | One zero-point `timeout` event, active streak 0, score remains 1050; answer/explanation and static timeout feedback are available. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-10` | With frozen `timerMode=off`, wait beyond the configured duration. | No timeout, attempt, score event, or streak reset is created; card remains unresolved. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-11` | From score 450, streak 4, choose abandon; reopen the partial result. | One zero-point `abandoned` event/reset and one partial result; score remains 450, no completion milestone is awarded, and reopening returns that record. |
[stderr] | `SCORE-FX-12` | Resolve a correct answer, then resend the same resolve command/interaction ID; enable mute and reduced motion before reopening feedback. | Exactly one attempt and score event exist; reopened feedback is static and text/non-colour equivalent remains complete with no audio required. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-011 — results and local milestone contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Result states
[stderr] 
[stderr] | State | Trigger and required stored fields | Available actions | Milestone effect |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `in_progress` | A run with frozen content/rules, score events, and a current safe boundary. | Resume, pause, abandon, return home only through a safe boundary. | None. |
[stderr] | `completed_result` | Last resolved attempt is continued; immutable `ResultSummary` has `runId`, `completionId`, answered, correct, score, best active-session streak, category, outcome counts, and action labels. | Replay (new run), choose theme, exit, reopen result. | Tutorial only: request `first_whistle_complete`. |
[stderr] | `abandoned_result` | Explicit abandon; immutable partial summary has the same fields plus `endedReason=abandoned`. | Resume only if a compatible safe snapshot precedes abandonment; otherwise replay, choose theme, exit, reopen result. | None. |
[stderr] | `recovery` | Draft is corrupt/incompatible or a write was interrupted; last confirmed snapshot is retained. | Resume confirmed, discard draft, return home. | None until a real completion commits. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] `answered` counts immutable resolved attempts. `correct` counts only `correct`.
[stderr] `score` and `bestActiveSessionStreak` copy the committed score-event ledger.
[stderr] The summary identifies the frozen category, not a current theme label that may
[stderr] change. It displays score, answered/correct, best active-session streak,
[stderr] category, and labelled replay/choose-theme/exit actions. It has no rank, RPG
[stderr] level, loot, social comparison, or account dependency.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Completion and milestone idempotence
[stderr] 
[stderr] Completion is a single atomic logical operation keyed by `(runId, completionId)`:
[stderr] write/retrieve the immutable `ResultSummary`, write/retrieve its aggregate
[stderr] history entry, and, only for a completed tutorial, write/retrieve
[stderr] `first_whistle_complete`. A duplicate command with that key returns the stored
[stderr] objects unchanged. A completion command for a different run ID is not a retry;
[stderr] it may create a distinct result and, for another tutorial run, returns the
[stderr] existing milestone rather than a second one.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The `first_whistle_complete` milestone is a presence state keyed to the local
[stderr] profile, not a counter. Its first stored value includes the source tutorial
[stderr] run/completion ID and completion timestamp. Later tutorial completions may add
[stderr] their own immutable history/result once, but cannot replace those provenance
[stderr] fields or create another milestone. Reopen, restart, failed UI acknowledgement,
[stderr] and recovery replay are reads unless they initiate a new run.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Result and lifecycle fixtures
[stderr] 
[stderr] | ID | Input / action | Expected deterministic state and idempotence result |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-01` | Complete a standard 10-card run, then reopen using its run/completion ID. | One `completed_result`, history entry, and score ledger are stored; reopen returns them without any duplicate completion or milestone. |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-02` | Complete `tutorial-v1` TUT-05, then repeat completion after restart with the same IDs. | Exactly one result/history entry and one profile `first_whistle_complete` presence state; retry returns both unchanged. |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-03` | Explicitly abandon after card 4, restart, and open its result. | One `abandoned_result`, zero-point abandon event, and no completion milestone; partial score/history is recoverable and no attempt is inferred for card 5. |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-04` | Pause at an unresolved card, background/terminate, restart, then resume and complete. | Restore the confirmed unresolved card with no abandon/blank/timeout event; later completion creates one result and eligible tutorial milestone only once. |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-05` | On final-card completion, result write is interrupted; restart with a valid prior confirmed snapshot and corrupt draft. | Enter `recovery`, retain snapshot, offer resume-confirmed/discard-draft/return-home; no result or milestone is claimed until a successful completion commits. |
[stderr] | `RESULT-FX-06` | Complete a tutorial, confirm deletion of local history/profile/snapshots, restart, and complete a new tutorial run. | Deletion returns to first launch and removes old local records only after confirmation; the new profile/run can create one new result and one new milestone, with no duplicate residual completion. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Release boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] Release validation requires `SCORE-FX-01`–`SCORE-FX-12` and
[stderr] `RESULT-FX-01`–`RESULT-FX-06`: exactly 18 fixtures. Wrong, blank, enabled
[stderr] timeout, and explicit abandon are all recoverable outcomes with a monotonic
[stderr] score and no duplicate completion. Pause, interruption, and recovery are not
[stderr] substitutes for those outcomes and cannot silently manufacture an attempt.
[stderr] # Accessibility, recovery, and target parity bible
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Purpose and non-negotiable invariants
[stderr] 
[stderr] This is the accepted design evidence for `GAME-009`–`GAME-012` and
[stderr] `GPI-012`–`GPI-015`. It governs the shared first-release game on responsive
[stderr] web, PWA, Android, and iOS. It does not approve an account, remote sync,
[stderr] target-exclusive content, a target-exclusive ruleset, or a target delivery
[stderr] matrix.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every target consumes the same published question revision, adjudication rule,
[stderr] score-event ledger, local-profile schema, confirmed-snapshot format, and
[stderr] result/milestone idempotency rule. An adapter may translate input, layout,
[stderr] installation, permission wording, or cache handling; it must not transform an
[stderr] answer, scoring outcome, round seed, content eligibility decision, or confirmed
[stderr] save. A target that cannot read a supported confirmed snapshot must preserve it
[stderr] and enter recovery; it must never replace it with an empty profile.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Essential meaning is always exposed in plain visible text and semantic state.
[stderr] Audio, colour, iconography, vibration, and animation may reinforce that meaning
[stderr] but are never its sole carrier. The normal round has no time limit; optional
[stderr] timer mode remains explicitly enabled and its availability does not alter the
[stderr] following equivalence requirements.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-012 — profile, settings, and four input mappings
[stderr] 
[stderr] First launch creates a local profile without sign-in and presents **Start First
[stderr] Whistle** and **Settings** as plain-language controls. Optional notification and
[stderr] diagnostic permission denial leaves the same tutorial, settings, saving, and
[stderr] return paths available. Settings include text-size, high-contrast presentation,
[stderr] reduced motion, cue volume/mute, captions/descriptions, and input help.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Shared action | Touch | Pointer | Keyboard | Gamepad |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Navigate / choose a control | Tap its labelled target; swipe only scrolls, never chooses. | Click its labelled target; wheel only scrolls. | Tab / Shift+Tab moves focus; arrow keys move within an answer group or ordered pair. | D-pad or left stick moves focus; no hover-only state is required. |
[stderr] | Select an answer / reorder | Tap option; drag is optional and has visible move up/down alternatives. | Click option; drag is optional and has visible move up/down alternatives. | Space or Enter selects; ordered pair uses documented arrow-key move commands. | South button selects; ordered pair exposes labelled move commands using D-pad. |
[stderr] | Confirm / continue | Tap **Submit**, **Continue**, or the named recovery action. | Click the same labelled control. | Enter or Space activates the focused named control. | South button activates the focused named control. |
[stderr] | Back / pause / open help | Tap visible **Back**, **Pause**, or **Help**; system back is an equivalent where available. | Click the visible named control. | Escape returns/backtracks where safe; P pauses; both are documented and focus remains visible. | East button returns/backtracks where safe; Menu/Start pauses; both are documented. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] No action depends on a gesture, hover, pointer precision, or platform system
[stderr] button alone. Back never discards an unresolved answer, a draft, or local data:
[stderr] it reaches the existing pause, confirmation, or recovery path. Focus order is
[stderr] prompt → instructions → answer controls → submit → explanation/secondary
[stderr] actions, with a persistent visible focus indicator; feedback moves focus to its
[stderr] heading once and does not steal it for animation.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Accessible feedback equivalents
[stderr] 
[stderr] The following table is the release copy and presentation contract. Screen-reader
[stderr] announcement is the stated text once per state change; captions/descriptions
[stderr] show the same essential message. Contrast mode preserves the text and semantic
[stderr] state rather than replacing it with a colour-only palette.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Meaning | Required visible and semantic text | Non-colour equivalent | Audio/caption equivalent | Reduced-motion path |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Category / question context | Category name and question number (for example, “World tournament history — Question 3 of 10”). | Named category plus a distinct pattern/label; never colour alone. | No cue is required; any entry cue caption repeats the category/context. | Static panel and label; no sweep required. |
[stderr] | Selected, unselected, and disabled answer | “Selected: [answer]”, “Not selected”, or “Unavailable until [condition]”. | Border, check/selection icon with accessible name, and text state. | No cue is required; any selection cue is captioned “Answer selected”. | Static state change. |
[stderr] | Correct, incorrect, blank, or enabled-timer timeout | Outcome name, points, streak result, canonical answer/explanation, and next action. | Labelled success/error/empty/time icon or pattern, not green/red alone. | Caption names the same outcome and score/streak information; mute changes no meaning. | Static feedback with no forced celebration, shake, flash, or countdown motion. |
[stderr] | Streak / score update | “Score: N. Streak: N.” and, when applicable, bonus/cap text. | Labelled streak marker and numeral; not a colour, pulse, or sound alone. | Caption names points, streak, bonus, or cap. | Static score/streak marker. |
[stderr] | Pause, saved, or recovery condition | State heading, what is retained, any risk, and named actions. | Icon/pattern is supplementary to the heading and action labels. | Caption/description repeats heading and named actions; no cue is required. | No auto-moving modal or animated warning. |
[stderr] | Offline, update, unavailable content, or unexpected error | Plain-language notice naming availability, retained progress, and next action. | Text heading and icon/pattern; status is not conveyed through network colour alone. | If a cue plays, caption repeats the notice; mute changes no path. | Static notice; no looping spinner as the only status. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] All minimum target contrast, text scaling, semantic-label, and touch-target
[stderr] requirements are subject to the selected OS/browser matrix. Until that matrix is
[stderr] approved, certification records the actual settings and assistive technology
[stderr] used rather than claiming a numeric platform conformance level.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-013 — recovery states and fault evidence
[stderr] 
[stderr] `SaveSnapshot` means an immutable confirmed snapshot. A draft is never a
[stderr] confirmed save. At tutorial completion, each resolved-question boundary,
[stderr] explicit pause, and completed result, the system attempts to write a new
[stderr] confirmed snapshot without mutating the previous one.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Recovery state | Entry condition | Required notice and actions | Preservation invariant |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `write_failed` | A confirmed-snapshot write fails. | “Progress could not be saved. Your last confirmed progress is safe.” Offer **Retry** and **Continue without saving**. | Retain and make recoverable the previous confirmed snapshot; do not imply that the current unresolved state is saved. |
[stderr] | `interrupted_resume` | App/browser/OS ends or backgrounds after a prior confirmed boundary. | “Continue your saved round?” Offer **Resume confirmed round** and **Return home**. | Restore the exact confirmed content revision, position, score ledger, and unresolved state; interruption creates no blank, timeout, abandon, or duplicate completion event. |
[stderr] | `draft_recovery` | Draft is corrupt, incomplete, or incompatible while a confirmed snapshot exists. | “A recent in-progress change could not be recovered. Your confirmed progress is safe.” Offer **Resume confirmed progress**, **Discard draft**, and **Return home**. | Keep the confirmed snapshot until the player explicitly deletes local data; never silently overwrite it with the draft or a new profile. |
[stderr] | `delete_local_data` | Player requests local-data deletion. | Confirmation names that local profile, history, results, and snapshots will be removed and cannot be resumed. Offer **Delete local data** and **Cancel**. | Only confirmed deletion clears those local records and returns to first launch. Cancel changes nothing; remote state is not implied. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Fault-injection evidence must cover: failed write at a resolved boundary;
[stderr] failed pause write; termination after confirmed pause; termination after a
[stderr] resolved attempt; corrupt draft with a valid snapshot; incompatible draft with a
[stderr] valid snapshot; retry succeeding after `write_failed`; and cancellation versus
[stderr] confirmation of local deletion. Each fixture asserts unchanged shared rules,
[stderr] no silent loss of confirmed progress, and no duplicated result/milestone.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-014 — offline, update, and error notices
[stderr] 
[stderr] The installed published content batch and current confirmed snapshot support a
[stderr] basic session offline. No notice may block a locally available tutorial or
[stderr] round merely because a network, optional permission, or diagnostic endpoint is
[stderr] unavailable.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Notice ID | Trigger | Player-facing notice | Required handling |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `OFFLINE-CACHED` | Network is unavailable and required installed content is present. | “You’re offline. Installed questions and saved progress are available.” | Start/resume remains available; suppress live-refresh expectation. |
[stderr] | `OFFLINE-CONTENT-UNAVAILABLE` | Requested content is not installed and cannot be fetched. | “This question set is not available offline. Try again when connected or choose an installed round.” | Preserve current confirmed save; offer installed content or return home. |
[stderr] | `UPDATE-STAGED` | A compatible content version is available during a round. | “An update is ready and will be used after this round.” | Freeze the active round’s content revision and rules; apply only between rounds. |
[stderr] | `CACHE-INCOMPATIBLE` | Cache cannot support the pending content version. | “An update needs a cache refresh. Your confirmed progress will be kept.” | Preserve/export the confirmed snapshot before reset; provide **Refresh cache** and **Return home**. |
[stderr] | `UNEXPECTED-ERROR` | A non-recoverable unexpected operation error occurs. | “Something went wrong. Your confirmed progress is safe.” | Offer **Retry** and **Return home**; optional minimised diagnostic consent is separate and denial changes no recovery route. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Six release fixtures are required: offline launch of First Whistle with cached
[stderr] assets; offline resume from a confirmed snapshot; unavailable non-installed
[stderr] content; compatible update arriving mid-round; incompatible-cache refresh with
[stderr] confirmed-save restoration; and unexpected error with diagnostic permission
[stderr] denied. Each verifies the exact notice, named action, preserved snapshot, and
[stderr] unchanged adjudication/score semantics.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## GPI-015 — target parity and certification matrix
[stderr] 
[stderr] The matrix states first-release parity requirements, not approval of a specific
[stderr] device, browser, orientation, performance budget, packaging route, store
[stderr] submission, crash provider, privacy declaration, or language set. Those remain
[stderr] human approval gates. “Required” means a candidate target must demonstrate the
[stderr] item before it can be certified in the eventual selected matrix.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Capability / certification evidence | Responsive web | PWA | Android phone | iPhone (iOS) |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Shared content, answer, score, save, recovery, result, and milestone semantics | Required; no web rule branch. | Required; no PWA rule branch. | Required; no Android rule branch. | Required; no iOS rule branch. |
[stderr] | Input and focus evidence | Keyboard, pointer, touch, and gamepad maps; responsive visible focus. | Browser/device-supported maps plus install-surface focus evidence. | Touch map and external keyboard where OS/device offers it; visible focus for non-touch navigation. | Touch map and external keyboard where OS/device offers it; visible focus for non-touch navigation. |
[stderr] | Accessible feedback evidence | Text scale, contrast, captions/descriptions, non-colour states, reduced motion, semantic labels. | Same shared feedback contract before/after install and offline. | Same shared feedback contract using selected OS accessibility settings. | Same shared feedback contract using selected OS accessibility settings. |
[stderr] | Offline/update evidence | Cached installed content/snapshot and notices where web delivery supports them. | Install, offline cache, staged update, and incompatible-cache recovery notices. | Packaged installed content, offline snapshot, staged update/recovery notices. | Packaged installed content, offline snapshot, staged update/recovery notices. |
[stderr] | Form-factor adaptation | Responsive phone, tablet, and desktop layout; no hidden critical control. | Responsive installed layout; no PWA-only content/mode. | Sufficient touch targets; orientation only after approval. | Sufficient touch targets; orientation only after approval. |
[stderr] | Certification record | Chosen browser/OS/device, input methods, assistive-tech/settings evidence, fixture IDs, defects, and sign-off. | Above plus install/offline/update evidence. | Chosen device/OS, packaging/store/privacy evidence, battery/thermal/size budget evidence, and sign-off. | Chosen device/OS, packaging/store/privacy evidence, battery/thermal/size budget evidence, and sign-off. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Certification is blocked, rather than waived, until the target matrix and its
[stderr] approval gates are selected. A failure on any target is resolved by fixing the
[stderr] shared core or its adapter; it cannot be resolved by altering a question,
[stderr] answer rule, score rule, save schema, or recovery guarantee for that target.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Release evidence checklist
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Record one successful keyboard-and-touch traversal of every critical action,
[stderr]   plus the gamepad equivalent on supported web/browser candidates.
[stderr] - Verify each feedback-table meaning with cue volume muted, high contrast,
[stderr]   enlarged text, captions/descriptions enabled, and reduced motion enabled.
[stderr] - Execute all eight GPI-013 fault fixtures and six GPI-014 offline/update/error
[stderr]   fixtures against each selected target’s applicable capabilities.
[stderr] - Compare serialized confirmed snapshots and resulting score/milestone records
[stderr]   across targets for the same round seed and input sequence.
[stderr] - Retain the selected target/device/browser/OS evidence and approval record;
[stderr]   no unselected target is represented as certified.
